{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user is executing a stitch task (#5166) from a code generation pipeline. The task is to implement multi-file headers and quiet/verbose control for `cmd/head` per srd018-head requirements R3.1-R3.4. Specifically:\n   - R3.1: When multiple files are given, precede each file's output with `==> FILENAME <==` header followed by a newline, with a blank line separating each file's header from the previous file's output.\n   - R3.2: When a single file is given, do not print a header by default.\n   - R3.3: `-q` or `--quiet` or `--silent` must suppress all headers, even for multiple files.\n   - R3.4: `-v` or `--verbose` must print headers even for a single file.\n   \n   Files to modify: `cmd/head/main.go` and `cmd/head/head_test.go`. Acceptance criteria require `go build`, `go vet`, and `go test` to pass.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - GNU coreutils head behavior for multi-file headers\n   - Differential testing via `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests` comparing Go binary against `ghead` reference\n   - SIGPIPE handler via `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n   - Byte-count parsing via `sizeparse.ParseWithOptions`\n   - Header mode: auto (headers when >1 file), always (-v), never (-q)\n   - The last -q/-v flag wins (same as -c/-n mutual exclusivity)\n   - File header format: `==> FILENAME <==\\n` with blank line before headers (except before first file)\n   - Git is managed externally — no git commands allowed\n   - E8: No function may exceed 40 lines\n   - Must write all files before building/testing\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd018-head.yaml`\n     - Full SRD specification for head utility\n     - R3 section defines multi-file output and header requirements\n   